\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Exact path projection and observables}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Use the \vocabk{retained vacuum embedding}{fdip-00HG} and the vacuum data of
\ref{fdip-00HB}. Let #{\Omega=\mathsf Q^{\mathbb R}} with its cylinder sigma-algebra.
The consistent vacuum finite-dimensional laws of \ref{fdip-00HB} determine, by \citet{Theorem 2.4.3, pp. 237-238}{tao2011measure}, a stationary
probability measure #{\mathbb P_0} on this space. Its one-time marginal is #{\nu_0}.
For #{\Omega_X=X^{\mathbb R}} with its cylinder sigma-algebra define}

##{ (\mathcal P\omega)(t)=P\omega(t),\qquad \overline{\mathbb P}_0=\mathcal P_*\mathbb P_0.}

\p{The coarse one-time law is #{\rho}. For every bounded measurable path observable #{F},}

##{\int_{\Omega_X}F\,d\overline{\mathbb P}_0=\int_\Omega F\circ\mathcal P\,d\mathbb P_0.}

\p{In particular, for #{0=t_0<t_1<\cdots<t_n}, #{\Delta_j=t_j-t_{j-1}} and bounded
Borel functions #{f_j:X\to\mathbb C},}

##{\mathbb E_{\overline{\mathbb P}_0}\prod_{j=0}^nf_j(x_{t_j})
=\langle\psi_0,M_{f_0\circ P}S_{\Delta_1}M_{f_1\circ P}\cdots S_{\Delta_n}M_{f_n\circ P}\psi_0\rangle.}

\proof{

\p{For each finite set of times, \ref{fdip-00HB} gives a Borel
probability on a finite-dimensional Euclidean space. These measures
are inner regular, including the unit point mass when #{s=0}.
Their compatibility is exactly the projection condition (2.6) of
\citet{Theorem 2.4.3, pp. 237-238}{tao2011measure}.
That theorem gives the unique law on the cylinder sigma-algebra.
Uniqueness and stationarity of the finite-dimensional laws give
stationarity of #{\mathbb P_0}.}

\p{The coordinate maps of #{\mathcal P} are measurable, so #{\mathcal P} is measurable
for the cylinder sigma-algebras. The pushforward integral identity follows first for
indicators, then simple functions and bounded limits, as in \ref{fdip-00G8}. All
operators in this formula are bounded. Its absolute value is at most
#{\prod_j\|f_j\|_\infty}. Substituting the lifted observables in \ref{fdip-00HB} and
applying the measurable pushforward identity proves the formula. The #{n=0} formula is
integration against #{\rho}; the #{n=1} formula is
#{\langle1,M_{f_0}A_{t_1}M_{f_1}1\rangle}.}

\p{For a single-time bounded multiplier, the exact relation is}

##{JM_f=M_{f\circ P}J.}

\tikzfigs{0.8}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  every node/.style={inner sep=4pt},
  map/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},shorten >=2pt,shorten <=2pt}
]
\node (a) at (0,0) {$L^2(\rho)$};
\node (b) at (3.8,0) {$L^2(\rho)$};
\node (c) at (0,-2) {$L^2(dq)$};
\node (d) at (3.8,-2) {$L^2(dq)$};
\draw[map] (a) -- node[above=2mm] {$M_f$} (b);
\draw[map] (c) -- node[below=2mm] {$M_{f\circ P}$} (d);
\draw[map] (a) -- node[left=2mm] {$J$} (c);
\draw[map] (b) -- node[right=2mm] {$J$} (d);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{A retained observable acts on the full configuration by pullback.
The square commutes for every bounded Borel #{f}; its bottom
multiplier uses #{f\mapsto f\circ P}, while #{P} maps fields
in the opposite direction.}

\p{The lifted multiplier preserves #{\operatorname{Ran}J} and its orthogonal
complement: apply the same relation to #{\bar f} and take adjoints. Thus it commutes
with #{E=JJ^*}. However, no such commutation with #{E} has been established for
#{S_t}. In general the displayed multi-time correlation differs from}

##{\langle1,M_{f_0}A_{\Delta_1}M_{f_1}\cdots A_{\Delta_n}M_{f_n}1\rangle.}

\p{The latter inserts projections #{E} on both sides of every interior lifted
multiplier, as follows by expanding each #{A_t=J^*S_tJ} and using #{J^*J=I}.
Equivalently it replaces each hidden conditional state by the conditional equilibrium
given the retained present. \ref{fdip-00HK} detects this change already at three
times by taking the middle observable equal to one and comparing #{A_{2t}} with
#{A_t^2}. The coordinate insertions in that example are justified by their Gaussian
moments or by bounded truncation.}

\p{Under the reducing condition of \ref{fdip-00HI}, every #{S_t} commutes with
#{E}, so the two multi-time formulas agree. Without that condition the original
operator string still defines all the exact coarse multi-time observations. Neither
the existence of #{\rho} nor a family of positive one-step operators supplies the
missing semigroup identity.}

}
